the City Council of Louisville Kentucky  is outlawing no-knock warrants nearly  three months after police there shot and  killed Brianna Taylor a no-knock warrant  allowed three plainclothes officers to  burst into her apartment while she was  sleeping national correspondent Jericka  Duncan spoke with Taylor's mother about  the new law and Jericka there's also  similar legislation in Congress correct  that's right  Anthony Rand Paul introduced that  legislation it's called the justice for  Brianna Taylor Act which would end these  warrants nationwide Taylor's mother who  told me her daughter would have been 27  years old last week said her daughter  was a loving person whose death could  have easily been prevented with a  unanimous vote late Thursday the  Louisville Metro Council passed  Brianna's law officially banning  no-knock warrants countywide attorney  Ben Crump and the law is only the first  step and getting justice but these  officers need to be terminated and  charged for this unnecessary and  justifiable killing of tameka's first  boy just after midnight on march 13th  police used a battering ram to enter  Taylor's apartment believing it was  being used by a drug dealer to receive  packages while the plain clothed  officers contend they identified  themselves Taylor's boyfriend says they  did not and he opened fire to defend  himself against what he thought were  intruders he later called 911 at least  eight times Taylor tonight autopsy  no she's a newly-released  incident report by Louisville Police is  mostly blank saying there was no forced  entry and marks Taylor's injuries as  none attorney lenita Baker not a single  officer has been charged and worse not a  single officer has been fired  Miss Palmer how are you coping with  everything are you exhausted I'm  definitely exhausted I hate what  happened she deserves justice do you  think she'll get justice I pray she does  but for now the family is grateful that  Taylor who was an emergency medical  technician is being honored by  Louisville slaw makers she's a blast  what she was here on earth and she  should continue to save lives with  Brianna's law they heard named Brianna  Taylor in the past police have said they  used these no-knock warrants to keep  suspects from fleeing the scene  destroying evidence or even firing at  police officers the mayor of Louisville  confirms he will sign this bill into law  as for that incident report that mostly  blank incident report police acknowledge  those errors and now say they're working  to correct them glad some remarkable  developments there in Louisville jerrika  thank you very much
 

